# Core (read first)
Layout: `playbook-*.mdc` = generic dev playbook (copyable to other projects). `project-*.mdc` = TravelAgency specifics (override the playbook where stated). Role subagents: `.cursor/agents/` (incl. `lead.md`, `architect.md`).

- Language: talk to the owner in Russian; agent-to-agent in English. Repo artifacts (commits, PRs, issues, comments, wiki, code comments): Russian.
- Inside `.cursor/` (rules, `.cursor/agents/`, templates): write what the process needs. Owner, lead, Cursor Automations, cloud agents, and role names are fine. `AGENTS.md` stays a short pointer to these files and does not repeat the rules.
- Outside `.cursor/` (wiki, issues, PRs, commits, comments, code comments, and any other public text): impersonal Russian by default. No personal names, no role labels as people, no «я/мы сделали», no `Co-authored-by` for bots. Write «принято решение», «добавлено», «проверено». When clarity needs it, name a configured automatic agent as a process actor (Cursor Automations, Bugbot, a cloud agent run): an automatic check left a finding, or an automatic agent pushed a branch. No personal names, and no names of retired chat-bots.
- No em dash (U+2014) in anything you write.
- Secrets: `playbook-security.mdc`. Never print, commit or paste one.
- Default at merge: if the owner already put the task in work or explicitly authorized that work, and the PR matches that scope, merge without a separate yes and notify the owner after merge.
- Ask the owner before merge only when the PR hits one of these and that exact case is not already authorized: irreversible or large-scale work (global delete, secrets, server changes, repo settings or access, history rewrite or force-push, spending money); Dependabot for docker or github-actions, including grouped updates that may contain majors; work outside the authorized scope; an unclear breaking change; a peer conflict; red CI.
- npm and NuGet Dependabot bumps, including majors: merge when CI is green and nothing needs clarification, then notify (`playbook-git-pr.mdc`). A peer conflict, red CI, or an unclear breaking change still blocks that merge.
- Team approval requests go through the lead.
- Results over process: read only what the task needs, minimal diff, stop at acceptance criteria, no gold-plating. Report: outcome first with links, then only decisions/risks.

Load on demand:
| Rule | When |
|---|---|
| `playbook-git-pr.mdc` | any commit, PR, review, merge |
| `playbook-workflow.mdc` + `playbook-step-0N-*.mdc` | feature work (doc-driven steps 1-8) |
| `playbook-security.mdc` | always before push |
| `playbook-cloud-agents.mdc` | launching or accepting a cloud agent |
| `project-travelagency.mdc` | facts, stack, roles, merge order |
